A||||17||pHoOl||Turbo questions||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||03-06-2001||09:41 PM||||Couple questions about turbo setups.<P>1.  How much faster if any does it burn out the engine?<P>2.  How much is the average used turbo unit?<P>3.  How easy is it to install?  I'm pretty good with directions and such, but i'm definately not a mechanic, and don't have a setup to take the engine completely out.<P>4.  Any changes that have to be made besides just adding it on?  or is it just plug and play?<P>I think this is all for now.  Thanks for help<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula 18||03-07-2001||01:30 AM||||answers:<P>1. I don't know. It would depend on the boost really. I think if you ra about 6-7 pounds you wouldn't notice it too much but if you ran like 22 you'd probally not make it out of the driveway. <P>2. A garrett T-3 off a Ford T-bird runs about 75-100 dollars where I live. They are a pretty good match to a 2.8L. <P>3. To do it from scratch you'd have to have pretty good fabraction skill and a good knowladge of how a turbo works. <P>4. Lots of other changes. It is no wear near as simple as plumbing a turbo into the exhaust. Fuel, timing, Compression, ect all have to be taken into consideration. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||03-07-2001||07:05 AM||||Of all high performance upgrades this is the one that will show the biggest increase in horsepower,it is also the one that will be the hardest one to accomplish because of the exhaust plumbing,lack of space,and cost.Good fabrication skills are a must!||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-07-2001||07:12 AM||||Unless your an expert at engine management and fabrication, dont try to make your own system, buy a complete system designed for your specific engine. The one I have on my 3.1 is same as for a 2.8, MSRP around $3,000. Gives it  real kick in the azz V8 type of performance. Ive noticed no problems in reliability in over 40K miles. (ie no oil cunsumption, reduction in power over time)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||03-07-2001||09:55 AM||||1) A properly tuned turbo setup won't wear the engine out any faster. An improperly tuned setup (one that detonates under boost) will destroy your engine very quickly.<P>2) Already covered<P>3) If you're definitely not a mechanic, you shouldn't try fabricating your own.<BR>If you decide to get adventurous and try anyway, this link has the closest thing to instructions for a DIY turbo setup I've seen: <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A>  <P>4) With a kit, no, because everything should be included in the kit.<BR>If you do it yourself you will have to add boost based fuel enrichment, boost based timing retard, 2 bar MAP sensor, etc. Check out <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org/gmecm/faq/ecm_selection.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.diy-efi.org/gmecm/faq/ecm_selection.html</A> <BR>and  <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A> <BR>for info on using a different chip for a turbo setup.||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||03-07-2001||12:21 PM||||one of our show cars here at the shop is a 3.4 Twin Dual Cam with a Garret T-3 Turbo. We can build your turbo setup inhouse but there are no immiediate kits as of yet, everything is built on a vehicle by vehicle basis. As well as the mechanicals we can proivide all of the neccassary fuel managemnet and hardware that make this a COMPLETE turn-key out of the door vehicle<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||03-07-2001||04:13 PM||||Does anyone knows if IRM still sells its turbo kit?<BR> That one kept the original exaust system (down pipe back at least) which I like more than the Design-1 system which dumps everything. I would like ot have one to be compatible with my FOCOA headers but that will be more rare. At least if I can keep my Borla I would be happy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Predator||03-07-2001||05:17 PM||||I have the Design One Systems Stage II kit. I installed it myself, but had to have it fixed due to other problems that crept up. I am like you in the sense that I can follow directions, and not a master mechanic. The kit is complete with everything you need including a map sensor, and computer upgrade to handle the timing and fuel mixture. The kit uses top grade parts and GM parts for the computer stuff. The exhaust is replaced if it is a manual, but kept the same if it is a auto. I have the manual, and got a new exhaust. It is louder, but it sounds good. It comes complete with the crossover pipe and the cat. The guy at Design One was really nice, and worked with me installing the kit through several phone calls, and was very patient about questions, and tried to explain them in non tech terms so I could understand what the kit was doing. The car is now complete and runs great. I have had no problems with it since. I highly recommend a kit over trying to fabricate one from scratch unless you really know what you are doing. It may cost more up front, but it will be cheaper in the long run if you end up blowing your motor , and have to have it rebuilt. I also passed my emissions test in Georgia with flying colors. That is something you want to take into consideration. If you don't have emission testing yet in the US you will. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Predator (edited 03-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroSEt||03-07-2001||09:55 PM||||Eric..... Do ya'll sell trhe cross-over pipe for the T-3 for the stock manifold's??? <BR>                        Thank's  Steve||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||03-07-2001||10:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>one of our show cars here at the shop is a 3.4 Twin Dual Cam with a Garret T-3 Turbo. We can build your turbo setup inhouse but there are no immiediate kits as of yet, everything is built on a vehicle by vehicle basis. As well as the mechanicals we can proivide all of the neccassary fuel managemnet and hardware that make this a COMPLETE turn-key out of the door vehicle<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What do you guys do for engine management?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mr.Frosty||03-08-2001||10:39 AM||||I have been following this subject for some time  with interest.My turbo was a custom job done by a professional mechanic friend of mine.I have been having trouble with detonation.I need to find out about an ECM<BR>chip or controller that will retard timing under boost.The motor ran real rich.Do I need a fuel pressure reg.for this app?Do those rice burner boost controllers help with this or are they even applicable here?<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||10
Z||000011||FieroSEt||03-08-2001||02:35 PM||||Does ANYONE make and sell the cross over pipe for the T~3 Turbo????????     <BR>                     Thank's Steve<BR>Mr Frosty...  Arw you running any kind of Performance Ignition... Such as the 6A or the 6AL??/  If YES . You can get a Boost Timing Master to help you maybe....   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||03-08-2001||02:43 PM||||MSD 6BTM, or Haltech E6GM will remidie your ignition problems. how ever are you sure your gettin genough fuel? is your f/pump old? stock injectors? high intake charge air temps? all these are factors to detonation and motor deterioration. <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  and find a dyno center near you. see if they have some kind of data aquistion system. to monitor your air/fuel ratio, manifold pressure, fuel pressure, EGT and intake charge air temps. find out why your cars knocking and fix it instead of throwing parts at it. that'll save you time and aggrivation.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||c m west||03-08-2001||04:36 PM||||like I said, we dont offer anything in a kit form persay - each customer brings their car here for an in-house modification and hot rodding, however that does not mean it is not possible, Chris here is the fabricator and would be able to answer that question, as far as fuel management Chris has worked out a system for his 3.4 Quad Cam with a T-3, to ask him directly, call 310-305-4111, or cwest@cwestco.com<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||gixxer||03-08-2001||07:32 PM||||Mr. Frosty<BR>The Design 1 Stage One systems use a special PROM chip and a new manifold pressure sensor that recognises boost conditions. According to Mr. Leslie, it retards the spark under boost. I presume it modifies the injector pulse width as well.<BR>The Stage Two systems also incorporate a knock sensor and a different computer. When I E-mailed Leslie if there was more to the stage two management strategy than just a knock sensor he clammed up tight. <BR>The general concensus about OEM knock sensors is, it's good as a fail-safe(backup), and that's about it.<BR>Presently, a GM knock sensor isn't good enough as a primary engine management device because of the excessive amounts of spark retard it subjects the engine to. Leading to high exhaust temps and shortened turbine life, as well as big drops in horsepower.<BR>If the stage two kit is just adding a fail safe, I will spend my $800 elsewhere.<BR>I've been looking into rising rate fuel regulators, and so far haven't seen anything yet in a bolt-in replacement that is a sure fire bet. Still trying to figure this one out and looking. Maybe Hypertech, Holley, AEM.<BR>The boost controllers, like from HKS<BR>(HKS EVC), look REAL GOOD, and I will probably fit one to my car when I get the X-tra money.<BR>Fortunately the V-6 is strong and will take considerable abuse, as you found out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroSEt||03-08-2001||08:19 PM||||Eric...   Thank's I'll get ahold of Chris and find out.       Steve||||reg||1
Z||000016||Predator||03-09-2001||10:31 AM||||I am not sure who Leslie is at Design One, but I talked to Kevin who is the owner when I bought my stage II kit, and it inlcudes more than just a knock sensor. The new ECM is included which takes into account the timing and fuel needed to run the turbo under no more than 10 pounds of boost. It produces 225 hp, and is reliable, streetable, and emmisions legal. I am not stating that it is the best solution, but Kevin knows what he is doing, and since I got the kit several mechanics have been impressed with the parts used, and the design of it. It is expensive, but it is complete with tech support with the install. For people like me who don't have the time, or equipment to custom fabricate plumbing, exhuast, and program ECM's it is a good deal. I wouldn't have a clue how to program a ECM to accomodate a turbo system, and wouldn't want to fry my engine finding out.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mr.Frosty||03-09-2001||11:39 AM||||Hey thanks guys for the responses.About the fuel pump it is a corvette fuel pump and the <BR>engine runs very rich(gas burning eyes from exhaust.So I figure it runs very rich at idle and very lean during boost.Kinda sucks.<BR>The power potential for this app is impressive,but only if you can tune it properly.I talked to Kevin Leslie at design one,and he was very helpful.He said the same thing about the stage two system.It has an ECM upgrade,knock sensor,wiring harness,& 2 Bar Map sensor.If it does what I need for it to do,then it is worth the price(keep things from blowing up)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> And I was throwing money at my car to get it to run properly before it gets rebuilt.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||12
